<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

Terra-Globals-Read: Lares (Tasked Spirit). Read-only diagnosis. Worktree: /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/b1-lift/wt (branch feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5&#45;-b1-lift, its HEAD). Write nothing. Mark claims MEASURED (file:line) or INFER.
packages/lararium-web/tests/e2e/glass-tabs.e2e.test.ts fails: "AssertionError: [first] Warning: Global assignment detected [name, onmessage, onmessageerror, cancelAnimationFrame, close, postMessage, requestAnimationFrame, onrtctransform, webkitRequestFileSystem, …]" — the FIRST tab's console carries the warning and the e2e treats a console warning as red. The same warning redded b1-lift's CI on glass-tabs at 4200215e7 and, on the span, the glass-edit-under-promotion e2e (the span runs the vessel on the page's main thread). Those names read as a dedicated worker's globals.
Find: who prints "Global assignment detected" (grep the repo and the TiddlyWiki5 submodule / tw5 plugin sources and any test harness), what it compares (a snapshot of globalThis before/after?), in which realm it runs, and what code assigns those names in the first tab's page realm since slice 4 (floor-tab.ts, ceremony-page.ts, main.ts's standFloorTab path, vessel-port.ts's vesselWorkerOfPort, the service worker broker registration). Name the minimal cure (the product stops assigning, or the detector's realm/allowlist reads wrong — say which with evidence) and its red. Final message under 600 words.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

